::GEO_U01_L05_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L05_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>What does MLA.GEO.FND.2 require students to construct? {
=Segment bisectors, angle bisectors, and perpendicular bisectors#Correct. The standard focuses on these bisector constructions.
~Copies of segments and angles only#Copying segments and angles belongs to a different construction standard.
~Rough sketches based on appearance#The standard requires precise constructions, not rough sketches.
~Definitions without drawings#Definitions help, but the standard requires construction.
}

::GEO_U01_L05_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L05_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement best describes a segment bisector? {
~A tool that measures degrees#A segment bisector is not a degree-measuring tool.
=A line, ray, segment, or point that divides a segment into two equal parts#Correct. A segment bisector creates two congruent segment parts.
~A ray that copies an angle#That describes angle-copy construction, not a segment bisector.
~A mark that labels one endpoint#Labeling one endpoint does not divide a segment.
}

::GEO_U01_L05_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L05_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>If M is the midpoint of segment AB, which statement is true? {
~AM = AB#AM is one part of AB, not the whole segment.
~MB = AB#MB is one part of AB, not the whole segment.
~A = M#Points are locations, not equal lengths.
=AM = MB#Correct. A midpoint divides a segment into two equal parts.
}

::GEO_U01_L05_Quiz_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L05_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which construction step helps locate the perpendicular bisector of segment AB? {
~Estimate the midpoint by sight#Sight estimation does not provide construction evidence.
~Measure &angle;A with a protractor#The task is constructing a perpendicular bisector of a segment.
=Draw matching arcs from A and B using the same compass opening#Correct. Matching arcs locate points equally distant from both endpoints.
~Rename segment AB as CD#Changing labels does not construct a bisector.
}

::GEO_U01_L05_Quiz_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L05_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>Why must the compass opening stay the same when drawing arcs from both endpoints of a segment? {
~So the diagram has more labels#Labels do not prove equal distances.
=So the arc intersections are based on equal distances from both endpoints#Correct. Equal compass openings support the bisector construction.
~So the line looks centered#Appearance alone is not construction evidence.
~So the segment changes length#The construction does not change the original segment length.
}

::GEO_U01_L05_Quiz_Q06::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L05_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement must be true for a perpendicular bisector of segment AB? {
=It crosses AB at its midpoint and forms right angles#Correct. Both the midpoint and perpendicular relationships are required.
~It passes through endpoint A only#A perpendicular bisector crosses the segment at its midpoint.
~It makes AB twice as long#The construction does not change the original segment length.
~It creates an angle copy#A perpendicular bisector is a segment construction, not an angle-copy construction.
}

::GEO_U01_L05_Quiz_Q07::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L05_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>A line crosses segment AB at point M. What additional evidence is needed to conclude that it bisects AB? {
~The line is drawn in a dark color#Color does not prove a midpoint relationship.
~A and B are labeled#Labels identify points, but they do not prove equal parts.
=AM = MB#Correct. Equal segment parts show that M is the midpoint of AB.
~The line is longer than AB#Line length does not prove that AB is bisected.
}

::GEO_U01_L05_Quiz_Q08::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L05_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>A line crosses segment AB at point M and forms right angles, but AM and MB are not shown equal. What can be concluded? {
~The line is definitely a perpendicular bisector#A perpendicular bisector also requires the midpoint relationship.
~M is definitely the midpoint of AB#Right angles alone do not prove equal segment parts.
~AB has been copied to a new ray#No segment-copy construction is described.
=The line is perpendicular to AB, but bisection is not established#Correct. Perpendicularity alone does not prove the segment is bisected.
}

::GEO_U01_L05_Quiz_Q09::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L05_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>What does an angle bisector do? {
=Divides an angle into two congruent angles#Correct. An angle bisector creates two equal angle measures.
~Divides a segment into two equal parts#That describes a segment bisector.
~Measures an angle with a protractor#Measuring is not the same as bisecting.
~Copies an angle to a new vertex#Copying an angle is a different construction.
}

::GEO_U01_L05_Quiz_Q10::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L05_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>If ray BD bisects &angle;ABC, which notation correctly shows the result? {
~AB = BC#Equal side lengths are not required for an angle bisector.
~m&angle;ABC = m&angle;DBC#The whole angle is not equal to one of its parts.
~BD = BC#The length of the ray is not the angle-bisector conclusion.
=m&angle;ABD = m&angle;DBC#Correct. The two smaller angles formed by the bisector are equal.
}

::GEO_U01_L05_Quiz_Q11::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L05_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which step belongs in an angle-bisector construction? {
~Mark the midpoint of a segment by sight#That is not an angle-bisector construction and is not precise.
=Draw equal arcs from the points where the first arc intersects the angle sides#Correct. These arcs help locate the bisecting ray.
~Change the compass opening for each side of the angle#Changing the opening breaks the equal-distance evidence.
~Erase the angle vertex#The vertex is needed as the endpoint of the bisecting ray.
}

::GEO_U01_L05_Quiz_Q12::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L05_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>Why is drawing a ray that looks centered not enough to prove an angle bisector? {
~The ray must always be horizontal#Angle bisectors do not have to be horizontal.
~The angle must be renamed first#Renaming does not prove bisection.
=Appearance does not prove the two angle measures are equal#Correct. Construction evidence is needed to support equal angle measures.
~A segment length must be copied first#Copying a segment is not required to prove an angle is bisected.
}

::GEO_U01_L05_Quiz_Q13::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L05_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which tool is used to draw arcs that support bisector constructions? {
=Compass#Correct. A compass draws arcs and transfers equal distances.
~Protractor#A protractor measures degrees, but it does not draw construction arcs.
~Calculator#A calculator does not draw geometric construction arcs.
~Notebook margin#A margin is not a geometric construction tool.
}

::GEO_U01_L05_Quiz_Q14::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L05_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which tool is used to draw the final line or ray through construction points? {
~Compass only#A compass draws arcs, but a straightedge draws the final line or ray.
=Straightedge#Correct. A straightedge connects construction points with a line or ray.
~Calculator#A calculator does not draw the construction line.
~Protractor scale only#A protractor scale is not used to draw the construction line or ray.
}

::GEO_U01_L05_Quiz_Q15::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L05_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>What is the best evidence that a constructed line is the perpendicular bisector of AB? {
~It appears to pass through the middle of AB#Appearance alone does not prove midpoint or right-angle relationships.
~It is drawn with a dark pencil#Line darkness is not mathematical evidence.
=It creates AM = MB and forms right angles with AB#Correct. A perpendicular bisector requires both equal parts and right angles.
~It has a label near AB#A label alone does not prove the required relationships.
}

::GEO_U01_L05_Quiz_Q16::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L05_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>A student constructs arcs from A and B using different compass openings. What is the likely issue? {
~The segment is automatically copied#Different compass openings do not copy the segment.
~The angle is automatically measured#A compass does not measure degrees.
~The labels become unnecessary#Labels still help communicate the construction.
=The construction may not locate a true segment bisector#Correct. Different openings do not preserve equal-distance evidence from both endpoints.
}

::GEO_U01_L05_Quiz_Q17::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L05_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>A student says M is the midpoint of AB because M looks centered. What is the misconception? {
~Midpoints cannot be constructed#Midpoints can be found through segment-bisector construction.
=Visual appearance is not enough evidence for equal segment parts#Correct. The student needs construction evidence that AM = MB.
~A segment cannot have endpoints#Segments have endpoints.
~Compass arcs cannot be used on segments#Compass arcs are used in segment-bisector constructions.
}

::GEO_U01_L05_Quiz_Q18::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L05_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which construction belongs in MLA.GEO.FND.2? {
=Constructing a perpendicular bisector of AB#Correct. Perpendicular bisectors are included in MLA.GEO.FND.2.
~Copying segment AB to a new ray#That belongs to the previous construction standard.
~Sketching a triangle by sight#A sketch is not a bisector construction.
~Naming an angle ABC#Naming an angle does not construct a bisector.
}

::GEO_U01_L05_Quiz_Q19::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L05_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which construction belongs in MLA.GEO.FND.2? {
~Copying &angle;ABC to a new vertex#That belongs to the previous construction standard.
~Measuring &angle;ABC with a protractor#Measuring an angle is not constructing an angle bisector.
~Drawing a rough angle that looks similar#A rough drawing is not a precise construction.
=Constructing a ray that bisects &angle;ABC#Correct. Constructing an angle bisector is part of MLA.GEO.FND.2.
}

::GEO_U01_L05_Quiz_Q20::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L05_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement correctly distinguishes a segment bisector from a perpendicular bisector? {
~A segment bisector must form right angles, but a perpendicular bisector does not#This reverses the special requirement.
~They are always unrelated constructions#A perpendicular bisector is a special kind of segment bisector.
=A perpendicular bisector bisects the segment and also forms right angles#Correct. A perpendicular bisector has both properties.
~A segment bisector only works for angles#Segment bisectors work with segments, not angles.
}

::GEO_U01_L05_Quiz_Q21::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L05_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>In a perpendicular-bisector construction, why are arcs drawn above and below the segment? {
=To create two intersection points that determine the bisector line#Correct. The line through the arc intersections is the perpendicular bisector.
~To make the segment longer#The arcs do not change the segment length.
~To measure the segment in inches#The construction does not require measurement units.
~To rename endpoints A and B#Arcs do not rename points.
}

::GEO_U01_L05_Quiz_Q22::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L05_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>In an angle-bisector construction, where does the final ray begin? {
~At a random point inside the angle#The final ray must begin at the angle vertex.
=At the vertex of the original angle#Correct. An angle bisector is a ray from the angle vertex.
~At the midpoint of one side#The midpoint of a side is not the starting point for the angle bisector.
~At the end of the longest arc#The ray starts at the vertex, not simply at an arc endpoint.
}

::GEO_U01_L05_Quiz_Q23::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L05_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>A diagram shows ray BD inside &angle;ABC and m&angle;ABD = m&angle;DBC. What can be concluded? {
~BD copies segment AB#The statement is about angle measures, not segment copying.
~BD is unrelated to &angle;ABC#Equal smaller angle measures show a relationship.
~AB = BC#Equal angle parts do not imply equal side lengths.
=BD bisects &angle;ABC#Correct. Equal angle parts show that BD is an angle bisector.
}

::GEO_U01_L05_Quiz_Q24::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L05_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>A diagram shows line â„“ crosses segment AB at M, AM = MB, and line â„“ forms right angles with AB. What can be concluded? {
=Line â„“ is the perpendicular bisector of AB#Correct. The line bisects AB and is perpendicular to AB.
~Line â„“ only copies AB#The diagram describes bisection and perpendicularity, not copying.
~Line â„“ is an angle bisector#The relationships are about a segment, not an angle.
~M is an endpoint of AB#M is the midpoint, not an endpoint.
}

::GEO_U01_L05_Quiz_Q25::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L05_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which explanation best supports a correct angle-bisector construction? {
~The ray looks close to the middle of the angle#Appearance alone is not enough evidence.
~The angle was labeled with three letters#Labels identify the angle but do not prove bisection.
=Equal compass arcs were used to locate a point that creates two congruent angles#Correct. Equal arcs support the angle-bisector construction.
~The ray was drawn with a straightedge only#A straightedge alone does not provide equal-distance arc evidence.
}

